{"chunk_id": "cbdd263916d8", "text": "Yudhishthira asks Bhishma a fundamental question: what truly constitutes righteousness, and whether it serves this world, the next, or both. Bhishma replies that righteousness is known through four sources\u2014the Vedas, the Smritis, the conduct of the virtuous, and the intention behind actions. He explains that dharma sustains social order and brings happiness in both worlds, yet it is subtle and situational. Through examples of thieves, hypocrites, and powerful men, Bhishma shows how people condemn faults in others while ignoring their own. Truth, non\u2011stealing, charity, and empathy\u2014treating others as oneself\u2014are presented as core principles. Fearlessness belongs to the pure-hearted, while wrongdoing breeds constant anxiety. Yudhishthira then challenges the explanation, arguing that duty cannot be determined by scripture alone because circumstances, especially times of distress, change what is right.\nYudhishthira asks the nature and sources of righteousness; Bhishma explains its foundations in Vedas, tradition, purpose, truth, empathy, and conduct amid life's moral complexities.\nThemes: nature of righteousness, sources of moral authority, truth and conduct, ethical reciprocity, moral hypocrisy", "vector": [0.022096576169133186, -0.0025001089088618755, -0.006198698654770851, -0.01783967763185501, -0.01929357275366783, 0.02495197206735611, -0.015573698095977306, -0.01175558753311634, -0.024545928463339806, -0.02167743444442749, 0.0169490035623312, 0.005497947800904512, 0.0008865809068083763, 0.0003773903881665319, -0.019070902839303017, 0.013726859353482723, 0.05757945775985718, -0.002599982311949134, 0.026130804792046547, -0.021769121289253235, -0.01021000649780035, -0.01121856365352869, -0.021454766392707825, 0.005091905128210783, -0.014394864439964294, 0.02878972887992859, 0.040237508714199066, -0.016346488147974014, -0.004531959071755409, -0.026248687878251076, 0.0017355046002194285, -0.007046803366392851, -